## Resume from directory entries

Advisory only. This is a redesign sketch, and it is not part of the plan.

Resume currently depends on the JSON status `linked` landing after `os.link` returns and before `os.replace`. A crash in that gap leaves `started` plus a backup name. An expired `started` entry is final and must not replace the warehouse, and a later phase B cannot `os.link` onto that existing backup. Holding the flock does not identify the gap, because the dead process has already released it.

Under the flock, before cleanup, use the directory entries:

- The backup inode equals the warehouse inode and `warehouse.copy.duckdb` exists. The link happened and the replace has not. `os.replace` the sibling onto `warehouse.duckdb`, fsync the directory, and write `swapped`. Do this when the record says `linked` and when it still says `started`. Keep the sibling and skip the copy.
- The backup inode differs from the warehouse. The replace already happened. Write `swapped` when the record is behind that, then delete a stray sibling.
- Any other shape keeps today's sweep, ladder, and phase choice.

`linked` can stay as the status written between the link and the replace, for doctor. It stops being the only key that preserves the verified copy. The extra work is one inode comparison on each acquired pass. A `started` record in that window finishes immediately, instead of waiting out the ceiling and then stopping for good. A file the user named themselves does not match `warehouse.issue147-swap.duckdb`, so the comparison does not swap their backup.
